The '''Cybersecurity and Privacy Initiative''' ({{Lang-bl|Iniciativa de Ciberseguridad y Privacidad}}) is a regulation in [[Law of Balisca|Baliscan law]] on {{W|data protection}}, {{W|cybersecurity}}, and {{W|internet privacy|privacy}} in the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. It is an important component of Baliscan privacy law and of human rights law, in particular [[Basic Law: Protection of Privacy|Article XIII]] of the [[Basic Laws of Balisca|Basic Laws]]. It also addresses the transfer of personal data outside of Baliscan territory.

The ICP's primary aim is to enhance individuals' control and rights over their personal data, protect {{W|internet privacy|internet anonymity}}, and to simplify the regulatory environment for international business. The ICP contains provisions and requirements related to the processing of personal data of individuals (formally called "data subjects") who are located in Balisca, and applies to any enterprise—regardless of its location and the data subjects' citizenship or residence—that is processing the personal information of individuals inside Balisca. Additionally, it established the [[Cybersecurity Agency (Balisca)|Cybersecurity Agency]] as a cabinet-level position within the [[Secretariat Council (Balisca)|Secretariat Council]].

Adoption of the ICP was in response to growing concerns in Balisca regarding the international expansion of {{W|surveillance capitalism}}, and privacy concerns stemming from {{W|data leak}}s such as the {{W|2021 Microsoft Exchange Server data breach}}, and misuse of data by corporations, such as the {{W|Facebook–Cambridge Analytica data scandal}}. The ICP is considered the cornerstone of the wider Baliscan shift to {{W|collaborative e-democracy}} and {{W|open-source governance}}. 

